WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:04.640
When you think of Leonardo da Vinci,&nbsp;
you probably think of the Mona Lisa.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.240
Leonardo da Vinci is one of the&nbsp;
most famous artists in the world.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.080
But he is much more than that.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.960
Indeed he never thought of himself as an artist.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:18.800
When he sent a letter to the ruler of Milan&nbsp;
describing his strengths when he was looking for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:24.320
work, he listed 10 different skills. And almost as&nbsp;
an afterthought, he mentioned he could also paint.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.920
The reason his artworks are&nbsp;
so realistic and detailed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:31.200
is that he invested much of his&nbsp;
time in the study of science.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.120
da Vinci was born in a village near&nbsp;
Vinci, just outside Florence, in 1452.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.880
The illegitimate son of a farmer’s&nbsp;
daughter and a prominent lawyer.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:46.640
Although he didn’t receive any formal education,&nbsp;
his artistic ability was clear from a very early age.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:51.840
When he was 15, he studied under the acclaimed&nbsp;
artist Andrea del Verrocchio in Florence.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.680
When da Vinci helped Verrochio paint&nbsp;
an angel in the “Baptism of Christ”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.960
the story goes that his version was&nbsp;
so much better than his master’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.480
that Verrochio vowed never to&nbsp;
pick up a paintbrush again.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.960
da Vinci was a notoriously slow painter&nbsp;
and many of his works were never finished.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.200
Only about two dozen survive.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.320
A drop in the bucket compared to other&nbsp;
artists. Van Gogh left more than 2,000 works.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.960
da Vinci wasn’t just interested in how the&nbsp;
human body or nature looked on a canvas.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.680
He wanted to know why they appeared as they did.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:24.960
He was able to give the Mona Lisa&nbsp;
that mysterious look on her face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.840
because he studied all the&nbsp;
muscles involved in smiling.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:28.320
He devoted a lot of his time&nbsp;
to studying human anatomy.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:33.120
If you wanted to learn about human anatomy&nbsp;
in the 1500s, there was no easy way to do so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.800
no medical textbooks and, of course,&nbsp;
no way to google search an image.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.240
So he studied the only way he&nbsp;
could - by examining corpses.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.760
Hospitals were keen to support his artistic&nbsp;
research and gave him access to cadavers.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.720
He dissected over 30 bodies in his lifetime and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:52.080
filled thousands of pages with&nbsp;
notes and detailed drawings.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:56.640
If you’re having trouble reading this, it’s&nbsp;
because da Vinci had a habit of writing backward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.960
He liked to go from right to left. He was&nbsp;
left-handed. So some have speculated this would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:06.160
have stopped his ink from smudging. His writing&nbsp;
can be read normally only if viewed in a mirror.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:10.400
He probably wouldn’t have done so if he&nbsp;
had any intention of publishing his notes.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.520
His depictions of the body are&nbsp;
beautiful. And for the most part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:18.960
accurate. So accurate that professors can&nbsp;
actually use his drawings to teach anatomy.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.760
One of his most famous works&nbsp;
that perfectly illustrates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.960
the fusion of art and science is the Vitruvian man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:29.760
which depicts the proportions of the human body&nbsp;
according to Vitruvius - a Roman architect.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.760
The man’s outstretched arms are equal to&nbsp;
his height, which is true for most people.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.440
He was particularly obsessed with&nbsp;
the heart which he described as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.320
a “...wonderful instrument&nbsp;
invented by the Supreme Master.”

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:45.280
Many of his medical conclusions about the heart&nbsp;
have turned out to be stunningly accurate.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:49.040
He was the first to describe the heart&nbsp;
as a muscle, that it has four chambers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.520
and that the arterial valves opened and&nbsp;
closed to let blood flow around the organ.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.040
He was also the first to&nbsp;
recognize coronary artery disease.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:02.080
Around 1506, he witnessed a 100-year-old&nbsp;
man die suddenly. He dissected his heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:06.640
and discovered that his arteries had narrowed.&nbsp;
He deduced, this was what killed him.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:10.880
He was far ahead of his time. Coronary&nbsp;
artery disease wouldn’t be described&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:14.320
by a physician until more than 150 years later.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:18.080
It’s a mystery why he never published any&nbsp;
of his work. His notes were not discovered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:23.520
until the late 18th century - more than 250&nbsp;
years after his death. Had they been known during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:27.920
his lifetime, they could have had a profound&nbsp;
effect on the understanding of the human body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:31.600
and, perhaps, helped physicians&nbsp;
find ways to heal certain ailments.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.760
In the same way that he studied the body to&nbsp;
improve his paintings and then became obsessed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.960
with anatomy, he studied plants to improve&nbsp;
his art and then became obsessed with botany.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:44.000
He drew most of his delicate works with red chalk.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.800
His sketches of plants were so accurate they&nbsp;
could have been considered scientific studies.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:52.560
In the Study of the Star of Bethlehem,&nbsp;
the swirls of leaves are similar to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.600
movement of water - another one of his obsessions.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:59.920
da Vinci dedicated a lot of time&nbsp;
trying to understand the flow of water.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:03.680
Most of his 72-page scientific&nbsp;
diary called the Codex Leicester&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:08.480
is dedicated to the study of the motion&nbsp;
of water in seas, rivers, and canals.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:13.840
Bill Gates bought it for nearly $31 million -&nbsp;
making it the most expensive book ever sold.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.120
da Vinci also invented the lock that almost any&nbsp;
canal or waterway you visit uses to this day.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:24.160
The lock was mitered - as in - two 45&nbsp;
degree angles meet each other at a point.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:27.840
His contribution to engineering&nbsp;
extended from the waters to the skies.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:32.480
Da Vinci sketched out this flying machine&nbsp;
that he called the Aerial Screw while he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:36.800
was employed as a military engineer&nbsp;
by the Duke of Milan in the 1490s.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:40.720
The device was meant to be powered by four&nbsp;
men who would push the four wooden shafts in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:46.160
a circular motion. da Vinci believed this would&nbsp;
generate enough force to lift it off the ground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:50.240
Had he actually attempted to create a real-life&nbsp;
model it would have been too heavy to lift.

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:55.600
But the basic principles of lift-off provided&nbsp;
the earliest foundation for modern helicopters.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:59.440
He also made sketches of a parachute,&nbsp;
speculating that if a person had a tent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:02.480
made from linen that matched their&nbsp;
proportions, they would be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:06.720
jump from any height without being injured.&nbsp;
Once again, he didn’t make a real-life model.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:12.880
But a British skydiver did use da Vinci’s idea 500&nbsp;
years later, in 2000, and it worked, in practice.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.840
da Vinci clearly had confidence in his own designs&nbsp;
and had done some mathematical equations.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:20.000
He applied math to his paintings.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.520
The Last Supper, one of the most&nbsp;
recognizable works in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:27.920
is a prime example of the use of the&nbsp;
mathematical principle of perspective.

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:32.400
Jesus is sharing a final meal with his&nbsp;
12 disciples before his crucifixion.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:36.560
All the lines in the painting converge in&nbsp;
one place, known as the vanishing point so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:41.120
that the focus is on Jesus. This was done&nbsp;
to emphasize the importance of Christ.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:43.520
And he wasn’t afraid to try new things visually.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:46.640
The traditional Florentine way of&nbsp;
painting was to outline an image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:52.320
giving it a very crisp appearance. Instead, he&nbsp;
perfected a new technique, sfumato,&nbsp;

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:56.400
which means “vanished” or “evaporated”&nbsp;
in Italian. It gets rid of hard edges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:59.920
by blending everything without&nbsp;
lines or borders. The Mona Lisa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:05.040
is a classic example of the use of sfumato,&nbsp;
particularly in the shading around the eyes.

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:08.560
He also began to experiment&nbsp;
with new materials for painting.

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:13.440
Renaissance period artists were moving away from&nbsp;
egg tempera paint - which was pigments mixed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:19.120
egg yolk and water to oil paints, which allowed&nbsp;
for more vivid colors and greater contrasts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:23.600
However, oil paints were complicated to&nbsp;
make and the quality fluctuated greatly.

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:28.480
So, da Vinci put on his chemistry hat and&nbsp;
cooked his own pigments in linseed oil at a low&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:33.840
temperature and added about 5% of beeswax which&nbsp;
prevented the paint from darkening on the canvas.

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:40.400
da Vinci’s art was a confluence of biology,&nbsp;
chemistry, engineering, mathematics, and physics.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:44.240
He employed all these skills when France’s&nbsp;
King Francis I appointed him as ‘the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:48.320
first painter, engineer, and architect&nbsp;
of the king’ when he was already in his sixties.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:54.000
His final work was not a painting but&nbsp;
a party he threw in the summer of 1518 

00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:00.560
at his residence at the&nbsp;Château du Clos Lucé, the king’s summer castle, in&nbsp;Central France, to thank him for his generosity.

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:06.320
There was a spectacle at the party where players dressed up&nbsp;
as planets surrounded by the sun and the moon.

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:11.280
da Vinci was the first to explain why you can see&nbsp;
light between the two points of a crescent moon.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:15.280
He figured out it was due to sunlight hitting&nbsp;
the Earth and reflecting onto the moon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:20.000
called Earthshine. This was an outlandish&nbsp;
theory at the time. Most people didn't even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:25.360
know that Earth orbited the sun! He was,&nbsp;
once again, far, far ahead of his time.

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:29.680
When da Vinci passed away at the&nbsp;
chateau on the 2nd of May in 1519,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:34.400
he left behind thousands of pages of&nbsp;
notes and drawings for future generations.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:36.160
He may be best known for his art.

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:40.880
But clearly, he was a Jack of All&nbsp;
Trades and the Master of Them All.

